WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:10.200
It is the failure of the Euro, threatening to bring
down the continent and the rest of the world.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.280
How did it happen?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.880
What’s happening? Failing currencies?
Collapsing banks.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.800
Whole countries on the edge of bankruptcy?

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.360
- The upside versus the downside
in Greece is pretty attractive.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.000
- A stressed investor who
truly understands great value.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.200
But the investors fill their pockets anyway.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.400
Have we lost control of the economy?

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.920
My name’s Amund.
I’ve always been fascinated by economics.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.800
How it affects society;
How it affects you and me.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.680
Is there an invisible hand
pulling the strings?

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.600
Or is it self-governing, based on principles
we neither understand nor control?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.440
How can we regain control?
That’s what I’ve been wondering about.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.800
That was how I discovered Bitcoin.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:05.400
Bitcoin is a decentralised payment system.
The first specially-made internet currency.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.480
100s of 1000s of computers around the world
work equally in a network...

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:16.560
...to create, move and record ownership
of the digital units known as Bitcoins.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.640
Bitcoins are programmable digital cash.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.120
You can send however much you want to
whoever you want, wherever you want...

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.920
...almost free of charge
and completely anonymously.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.200
Do we really need a new currency?

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.600
A revolution? Fine!
But is it good or bad?

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.680
Since 2009, the value of the bitcoin
has varied by several million per cent.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.000
The currency has been linked with
criminal networks and shady business.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:14.400
Neither the Norwegian authorities nor others
welcome the revolution.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:29.960
Despite the warnings, bitcoin has gone from
obscure programme to billion industry.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:35.440
Many people have embraced the technology.
what makes it so attractive?

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.600
I’ve had one bitcoin and it does work.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:36.240
Satoshi Nakamoto...
It’s a name I’m going to hear many times.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:57.800
That’s when I decided.
I had to see how bitcoin actually works.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.520
My itinerary’s ready. My digital wallet full.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.160
I’m off!

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.440
Is Scandinavia ready for Bitcoin?

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:11.680
Day one. Forget Bitcoin.
Cash is King.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:38.760
What’s needed for garages
and other shops to accept Bitcoin?

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:46.040
Could the answer lie in an office block outside Copenhagen?

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.480
- What is Coinify?

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:57.240
- It’s a means of payment for
companies wanting to take Bitcoin.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:10.400
We have all sorts: Food, clothes,
internet services, electro etc.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:18.720
We’re the bridge between the old
financial world and Bitcoin.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.680
- A customer want to buy something
from a shop with Bitcoin.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:32.160
Bitcoin goes via Coinify who returns
it to the shop in Euros or Kroner..

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:38.160
There are a number of advantages.
The shop keeps accounts as usual...

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.600
...they pay no fees to accept
payments from Bitcoin...

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.640
...there are no chargeback frauds
as with credit cards...

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:53.240
...and it’s a one-click payment,
so it’s easy.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.560
- Do you feel that Coinify
is supported by the Danish authorities?

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:05.520
- Three years ago, laws about Bitcoin
were uncertain in Denmark and Europe.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:13.600
Now it’s better. We know the legal
position for Bitcoin in most areas.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.560
It can still get better,
but it is pretty good.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.560
- The Danish authorities are positive.
What about the rest of Scandinavia?

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:31.640
- Finland yes, Sweden iffy, Norway no.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:35.720
- What makes it so difficult
to regulate Bitcoin?

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:42.040
The question brought me here.
The Copenhagen docklands.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:15.320
Can Bitcoin and the financial world
live in harmony together?

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:22.200
Will Scandinavian countries give
Bitcoin and digital currencies a chance?

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:26.560
I think I’m ready for a beer!

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:43.800
- What do you think
will be the future of Bitcoin?

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:46.760
- Maybe I should talk a bit
about the past?

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:57.080
When Bitcoin came out in 2008-9,
it seemed crazy.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:07.080
I was interested because the core of Bitcoin
is a code which I wanted to try to break.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:17.200
Many have tried but none succeeded.
So Bitcoin could be here to stay.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:24.240
- What about Bitcoin and society?
Is it good news or bad news?

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:33.120
- You can control all your money;
You don’t need a 3rd party or bank.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:36.520
- The only way to get out of the boot
of government is to build a system...

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:43.400
...a currency that works completely
outside centralised control.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:47.560
And that is what we have today.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:52.240
It’s dissemination of power.
A paradigm shift.

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:21.040
Gothenburg.
Home to Volvo, Ullevi, Liseberg.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.640
And Bitcoin pioneers.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:37.440
The usual suspects.
The traffic dept. still has some way to go.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:09.600
How many have taken the leap?
How big is Bitcoin really?

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.320
Currently there are about 100,000
Bitcoin transactions per day.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:21.400
In comparison, Visa alone has
4,000 transactions per second.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:31.440
Can Bitcoin challenge the banks’ monopoly?
Will the system work on a large scale?

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:41.920
Then there must be more places like Webhallen:
The first big chain store to take Bitcoin.

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:37.480
Every day 200,000 Bitcoins change hands.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:46.120
Only 2.5% is used in shops.
That’s around 1.2 million US dollars.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:54.640
A drop in the ocean against USD 60 billion
in retail trade every day.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:59.480
You have to start somewhere.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:36.640
The block chain. A system for transactions
with no central authority.

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:42.600
Buying and selling, work contracts,
voting slips – everything can be stored here.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.520
With no bureaucracy or state control.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.960
That’ll be a challenge to society.

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:56.640
Maybe on a deeper level
than we might suppose.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:05.000
A Ugandan Bitcoin trader explains why
East Africa is ready for Bitcoin.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:11.160
- We don’t have PayPal or credit cards
like other countries.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:15.080
We use mobile phone transfers.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:20.280
It is a little bit like Bitcoin.
In the future all of us will use Bitcoin.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:22.560
- It’s about democracy.

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:52.520
Can Bitcoin democratise society?
Many people think so.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:25:00.240
The Swedish Bitcoin Party for example.
We’re to meet outside the Riksdag.

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:58.840
Freedom is built into the block chain
where all Bitcoin transactions are recorded.

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:06.000
Single transactions are collected in blocks
which the network registers every 10 mins.

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:14.440
The blocks form a chain all the way back
to the first signed by Nakamoto in 2009.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.640
There’s something religious about it.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:29.320
Maybe that’s why I’m here in Sollentuna,
outside Stockholm. Looking for...

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:32.600
- Roberth!
- Hi!

00:30:56.360 --> 00:31:04.280
A democratising technology; a shared
value system; a people’s currency.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:09.800
Bitcoin must be good, mustn’t it?

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:20.760
But banking and finance are resisting.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:26.080
Are they scared of losing control?
Or are they seeing something I’m not?

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:47.720
Boden, Nordbotten.
80km south of the Arctic Circle.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:53.960
Is it here I will find the answers?

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:04.040
I’m going into an old helicopter hangar.
And I think I know what’s hiding there.

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:14.400
It’s here I’m hoping to find
the heart of Bitcoin.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:21.440
- Welcome to KNC Clear Sky Farm.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:32.440
Physically I can’t get any closer to Bitcoin.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:37.480
In this hall alone, there are
43,000 specialised computers.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:44.600
It’s here the value of Bitcoin lies.
This is where the block chain is created.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:47.080
What is the motivation?

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:50.560
- Who is behind it?
- You better talk to Sam.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:59.640
- What is Bitcoin mining?
- Bitcoin mining is transaction processing.

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:09.640
- If someone wants to send a Bitcoin
from A to B it must be guaranteed.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:13.120
We don’t want person A to be able
to use the same Bitcoin twice.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:22.360
So you must be able to trust the network
which guarantees the money is real...

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.680
...that the Bitcoin won’t be sent elsewhere
and the transaction will be confirmed.

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:34.040
The miners create blocks of transactions
and have a mathematical problem to solve.

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:36.920
When they solve the problem,
they announce to the network:

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.920
”“I have solved it and included all these
transactions in my block.”

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:44.080
So Bitcoin mining is really
transaction confirmation.

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:47.120
- How are new Bitcoins put into circulation?

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:54.200
- As soon as you have created a new block
full of transactions, you get 25 new Bitcoins.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:57.680
And you get to decide where they go.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:01.120
We send then to ourselves first,
then to our customers.

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:04.040
- So that is the motivation for
creating this centre?

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:12.560
- Imagine 3,600 new coins per day; the more
computer power you have, the more you get.

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:16.000
If you only have one computer
you don’t stand a chance.

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.880
So you have this hypergrowth
of this Bitcoin network.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.360
- How secure is the network?

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:26.120
- The more computer power in the chain,
the more secure the chain is.

00:34:26.200 --> 00:34:32.600
If you want to take a transaction out,
you would need 4 billion laptops...

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:36.200
...and you would have approximately
10 minutes to do it.

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:39.280
- How many laptops do you have
in your facility?

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:43.240
- Laptops don’t work for us.
We have very specialised chips.

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:48.520
The technology we use is cutting edge.
It is beyond mobile phones.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:50.520
It is the bleeding edge
of the semi-conductor industry.

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:57.120
Bitcoin is the world’s most secure network .
Nobody has greater computer power than us.

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:05.560
The world’s most secure network?
Can that be correct?

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.560
So Bitcoin is safer than the bank.

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:37.240
In terms of computer power, it’s the
world’s fastest growing payment network.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:42.880
How come such cutting edge technology
has wound up in the Swedish countryside?

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:36.680
Is that to be the conclusion from
this experiment?

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:44.920
2,500 km for a cake and a ‘Sorry, no’?

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:46.760
No!

00:38:49.600 --> 00:38:52.520
Maybe Scandinavia’s not ready for Bitcoin.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:57.760
It’s not easy to hand power and control
to technology from an unknown source.

00:38:58.280 --> 00:38:59.720
But one thing is for sure:

00:38:59.800 --> 00:39:04.480
We have got a technology that will
revolutionise how we act together.

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:06.360
It is up to us to use it.

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:11.120
To democratise the economy
and thus create a better society.

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:13.440
That way we can all be Satoshi Nakamoto.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:16.280
That way we can all be Satoshi Nakamoto.

